## Scope clarification: upgrade UX and developer impact

#299 owns the finalized product upgrade/recovery/deprecation behavior built on #297/#298.

A BaseHarbor update must be able to distinguish and communicate:

- BaseHarbor/CLI version change;
- runtime image/API change;
- capability contract change;
- selected/default provider product/version change;
- state/schema migration;
- repository workload rebuild requirement;
- persistent-data impact;
- rollback/recovery classification;
- application action required vs no application change required.

The normal output should be concise but must not hide application-relevant changes. Verbose/JSON/MCP may expose exact image references, digests, migration IDs and compatibility details.

Example semantic distinction:

```
Application capability: SQL unchanged
Provider realization: PostgreSQL 18.x -> 18.y
Application impact: SQL contract unchanged
```

A safe upgrade may automate operational work, but must surface meaningful compatibility/deprecation information before/while mutation according to risk. Success still requires post-upgrade verification.
